# Options the spec does not have, which macOS and GNU ls take as extensions (-b -e -h -l@ -Q -T -U -v -w -W -X -Z ...).
-X
-W
-w
-v
-Q
-lZ
-lUu d
-lU
-lT
-lh
-l@
-e
-b
# -R with a directory operand: ours (and GNU) write the "d:" header for the first directory too, as the
# text of the spec has it ("each list of files within a directory shall be preceded by" the header, when
# -R makes more than one directory be written). macOS leaves the header off the directory named on the
# command line, which is how the spec's own EXAMPLE shows it. Everything below the first line is identical.
-R
-R .
-R d
-R d/sub
-R e
-R nodir
-R -a d
-R -A d
-R -p
-Ra
-RA
-RF
-RF d
-RL
-RL d
-RH dl
-RLl d
-Rl d
-lR
-lRa
-lRF
-lRL d/sub
-l -R
# -1 does not turn the long format off (spec, OPTIONS); macOS's does.
-l -1
# the last of -A and -a decides (spec; PCTS 97); macOS lists . and .. whichever comes last.
-aA
# -u and -c alone sort by access / status change time in GNU ls; POSIX defines them as the time -t sorts
# by and -l shows, nothing more. macOS and ours keep the name order.
-u
-u d
-c
# -f turns off -r -S and -t, even those that follow it (spec; PCTS 92). GNU lets them sort again.
-ftSr
-fltsr
# -C and -x: the column layout is unspecified except that the width of the columns is constant. Ours makes
# every column as wide as the longest name plus two, GNU fits each column to its own names.
-x
-lx
-C
-lC
# -m: ours fills a line up to the width (80 characters, the same as GNU); macOS breaks one name earlier and
# leaves a blank at the end of the line.
-m
-1 -m
-lm
# -lF on a link: the sign goes after the pathname of the link ("sublink@ -> sub"; spec: -F writes an
# at-sign after each pathname that is a symbolic link). GNU appends the sign of the target to the
# target instead ("sublink -> sub/").
-lF
-lF d
-lF d/sub
-ldF f1 f3 ff d t lf ld dang
# -R with a link operand: the "ld:" header of the first directory (see the other -R entries).
-R ld
-RL ld
-RH ld
# macOS writes an empty "total 0" before it says it cannot read the directory.
-l nodir
# -H and -L evaluate the file a link on the command line refers to (spec). macOS ignores -H when -l is
# given and shows the link; for a link that leads nowhere both macOS and the spec's PCTS 44 differ: ls -L
# must write a diagnostic, ours does, and neither -H nor -L may list the name of a file it cannot evaluate.
-lH lf
-lH dang
-lL dang
-L dang
-H dang
# A link inside a listed directory that leads nowhere (PCTS ls 69, GA53): with -L the file it refers to has to be
# evaluated, which cannot be done, so ours writes a diagnostic, exits 1 and leaves the entry out, the same way macOS
# does for -R (-L alone only needs the names, so all four list the link). With -l macOS leaves the entry out in
# silence and exits 0, GNU lists it with question marks.
-RL dl
-lL dl
